  "summary": "New spatial and multiome models infer domains, interfaces, and regulators directly from molecular data, while cohort-scale models link cell programs to disease.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is structured, often interpretable modeling that extracts biological organization directly from molecular measurements. In spatial data, spSheaf applies cellular sheaf theory to raw transcripts without cell segmentation, SCOUBI uses a neural network on extrasomatic RNA to map axon-dendrite interfaces, and SPIN couples spatially informed co-expression networks to Bayesian regression for population-scale clinical prediction. For multimodal data, Bloom learns shared representations from paired expression and accessibility in ~300k mouse brain cells, and scMINA offers an XAI-based multiome workflow for regulatory prioritization. SEMPER aligns protein language model embeddings with cell-type-specific interaction networks. TriSCOPE links cell-type programs to genetics across ~4.2M nuclei from 627 Alzheimer's donors. The items are heterogeneous, with brain-focused work prominent.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
